---
metadata:
  layer: architecture
  scope: docs/architecture/
  version: "1.0"
  description: "Architecture documentation structure and conventions"

template:
  required_directories:
    - "drafts/"
  single_source_of_truth:
    - source: "Architecture documents"
      for: "system design decisions, patterns, trade-offs"

agent_rules:
  document_lifecycle:
    state_field: document_state
    states:
      - id: draft
        description: "Design is being explored"
      - id: review
        description: "Design is under review"
      - id: accepted
        description: "Design is approved but not necessarily implemented"
      - id: current
        description: "Document is a current architecture source of truth"
      - id: superseded
        description: "A newer design or architecture document replaced this one"
      - id: archived
        description: "Document remains available as history"
    initial: draft
    terminal: [archived]
    transitions:
      - {from: draft, to: review}
      - {from: review, to: draft}
      - {from: review, to: accepted}
      - {from: accepted, to: current, requires: ["implementation verified", "current architecture updated"]}
      - {from: accepted, to: superseded}
      - {from: current, to: superseded}
      - {from: superseded, to: archived}
  update_order:
    - "Create/update draft documents when designing"
    - "Reference drafts from Epic and Story documents"
    - "Update the current architecture source when implementation is verified"
    - "Archive only superseded documents that retain historical value"
  reference_policy:
    target_must_exist: false
    allow_external: false
    require_description: true

strong_references:
  - type: upstream
    target: "/docs/prd/epics/"
    sync: auto_pull
    validation: strict
    description: "Architecture designs support Epic requirements"
  - type: downstream
    target: "/docs/modules/"
    sync: auto_push
    validation: loose
    description: "Architecture decisions reflected in module docs"
---

# Architecture

Design documents: drafts area, design-decision sources of truth, and a
lifecycle that separates "design accepted" from "design implemented".
